# Install with: sudo install -m 440 -o root -g root sudoers.d/homelab-deploy /etc/sudoers.d/homelab-deploy
# Always validate with `visudo -cf` before installing — a syntax error in
# /etc/sudoers.d/ can lock out sudo entirely.
#
# This grants everyone in the `homelab-deploy` GROUP — not a specific
# user — exactly one capability: running /opt/deploy/deploy-executor.sh as
# root, with any arguments, no password prompt. Authorizing by group
# rather than by username is what lets you give each agent its own Unix
# account and SSH key (for a distinct identity in the auth/sudo logs, and
# so revoking one agent doesn't touch the others) without granting or
# revoking permissions per account: add an account to this group and it
# has exactly this capability; remove it and it has none, with nothing
# here to edit either way.
#
# The "any arguments" part is intentionally broad: target/branch names are
# dynamic, and trying to enumerate every exact (target, branch, env-file)
# combination here would be unmaintainable as targets or agents are added.
# The actual enforcement lives inside that script: it independently
# validates all three arguments against its own hardcoded per-target table
# before doing anything, and never passes them through a shell (no eval,
# no bash -c, no string-built commands) — so there's nothing for this
# wildcard to let through unsafely. This is the standard, documented
# sudoers pattern for a command that does its own argument validation.
#
# No account in this group should also be a member of the `docker` group —
# that would make this rule pointless, since group membership there alone
# already grants full daemon control.

# SSH exec_command sessions (what the MCP server uses) have no controlling
# tty. Some distros default to `Defaults requiretty`, which would make sudo
# refuse to run at all in that context — this line overrides that for
# every account in the group.
Defaults:%homelab-deploy !requiretty

%homelab-deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/deploy/deploy-executor.sh *
